WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.120
So, story time. When I first started getting started with coding,

00:04.120 --> 00:07.200
I was one of those people who thought that JavaScript,

00:07.200 --> 00:08.340
well, in order to know JavaScript,

00:08.340 --> 00:09.520
you need to learn Java.

00:09.520 --> 00:11.880
And this was back in the day where I was learning from an O'Reilly book,

00:11.880 --> 00:14.920
I picked up like an HTML book that my dad had kind of left around.

00:14.920 --> 00:16.840
And then so, when I saw the JavaScript,

00:16.840 --> 00:18.360
I was like, I picked up the Java book instead,

00:18.360 --> 00:19.520
and then I got really confused,

00:19.520 --> 00:23.000
and then I gave up and I was discouraged for many, many years.

00:23.000 --> 00:25.160
But then when I started getting back into it,

00:25.160 --> 00:27.360
then I started learning, okay, so it's different.

00:27.360 --> 00:29.040
And I started working with jQuery,

00:29.040 --> 00:30.000
and then at the round this time,

00:30.000 --> 00:31.840
frameworks are starting to become really big.

00:31.840 --> 00:36.640
So, of course, Angular at this point had really started to get really popular at that point,

00:36.640 --> 00:38.440
and then React came on the scene.

00:38.440 --> 00:41.600
And so, at this point, I'm thinking, okay,

00:41.600 --> 00:43.560
how am I going to get my skills up to date?

00:43.560 --> 00:45.560
And so, I kind of worked with all of these,

00:45.560 --> 00:48.720
and React was the one I'd really been pushing for at one of my old jobs,

00:48.720 --> 00:52.000
when I really first started getting hands-on experience with frameworks.

00:52.000 --> 00:54.880
Because after all, this was at least as far as the statistics show,

00:54.880 --> 00:56.120
the most popular one,

00:56.120 --> 00:58.480
and it gave me a lot of opportunities to practice JavaScript,

00:58.480 --> 00:59.800
so why not?

00:59.800 --> 01:02.120
But what's interesting is that at this company,

01:02.120 --> 01:03.760
even though I was pushing for React,

01:03.760 --> 01:07.360
I had my team going, well, there's this new kid on the block called Vue,

01:07.360 --> 01:09.080
and this is the round time Vue 2 had come out,

01:09.080 --> 01:10.520
that we should really check this out.

01:10.520 --> 01:13.200
It's supposed to be basically just as performant as React,

01:13.200 --> 01:17.520
and it has a more approachable way of building apps.

01:17.520 --> 01:19.680
And for me, I was like, I want to learn the most popular thing,

01:19.680 --> 01:23.080
right, the most cutting edge, so I was incredibly resistant to it.

01:23.080 --> 01:25.760
And so, part of the reason I tell this story is because I want you to know that

01:25.760 --> 01:27.000
this is where I came from,

01:27.000 --> 01:29.880
as far as like, experience working with all these different frameworks,

01:29.880 --> 01:33.920
and then from this context of switching from React to Angular,

01:33.920 --> 01:35.720
into the Vue ecosystem.

01:35.720 --> 01:37.240
And yeah, so we'll go from here.

01:39.720 --> 01:44.520
Okay, so just to make sure we clarify the simple definition of what Vue is,

01:44.520 --> 01:46.160
I think the simplest way to think about it is,

01:46.160 --> 01:49.400
it's a JavaScript framework for building web user interfaces.

01:49.400 --> 01:50.640
That's basically what it is.

01:50.640 --> 01:52.400
It can certainly be used to power other things,

01:52.400 --> 01:55.000
you know, now that we have frameworks that can help you build mobile apps,

01:55.000 --> 01:57.000
you can certainly use it underlying,

01:57.000 --> 01:59.400
but primarily, it's used to build web interfaces.

02:01.960 --> 02:04.280
Now, of course, the big question here is why Vue.js?

02:04.280 --> 02:06.200
Why would people consider using Vue?

02:07.480 --> 02:10.120
The first thing is that it's designed to be approachable,

02:10.120 --> 02:13.080
and what does that mean when we talk about approachable?

02:13.080 --> 02:17.160
And so, this is taken from the docs in that Vue built on top of standard

02:17.160 --> 02:21.680
HTML, CSS, and JavaScript with intuitive API and world-class documentation.

02:22.680 --> 02:24.800
For those coming from the React world,

02:24.800 --> 02:26.600
one of the things you'll know is that,

02:26.600 --> 02:31.000
because one thing React does is it goes all in on your ability to know JavaScript, right?

02:31.000 --> 02:33.040
You have to be able to write basically everything in it,

02:33.040 --> 02:36.200
and things have sort of gotten improved since then,

02:36.200 --> 02:37.360
but especially in the beginning,

02:37.360 --> 02:40.520
you were sort of kept to the conventions of JavaScript

02:40.520 --> 02:41.760
when like you were writing JSX,

02:41.760 --> 02:43.640
so you're writing HTML in your JavaScript,

02:43.640 --> 02:46.320
so you had to do things like class name instead of just class,

02:46.320 --> 02:48.000
because those are like reserved words.

02:48.080 --> 02:51.400
And you found yourself basically contorting the entire website

02:51.400 --> 02:54.160
into what made sense in JavaScript.

02:54.920 --> 02:58.240
And Vue's approach was that why not take what people already know,

02:58.240 --> 02:59.840
HTML, CSS, JavaScript,

02:59.840 --> 03:03.440
and let's just basically build on top of that instead.

03:03.440 --> 03:05.360
And so, as a result, you get a different learning curve,

03:05.360 --> 03:07.480
because I know that when I started with React,

03:07.480 --> 03:10.680
I felt like the amount of JavaScript I needed to really get productive

03:10.680 --> 03:15.000
was super high in terms of knowing a lot of either functional programming

03:15.000 --> 03:17.640
or a lot of the methods in order to loop between elements.

03:17.640 --> 03:20.680
Like, there was a lot going on as far as what's happening in React.

03:20.680 --> 03:22.520
So, there's a decent onboarding curve.

03:22.520 --> 03:25.400
But with Vue, I found that within the first couple of hours,

03:25.400 --> 03:27.480
I was able to get not only onboarded,

03:27.480 --> 03:31.000
but I've taught people who have been out of practice for coding for a long time,

03:31.000 --> 03:32.880
and they got up and running real quick.

03:32.880 --> 03:35.160
And so, we'll get to, I'll show you exactly how that goes,

03:35.160 --> 03:37.040
and we'll get to experience that yourselves.

03:37.040 --> 03:40.720
So, this is one of the things to consider when it comes to Vue.

03:42.120 --> 03:45.400
Performance is always a big thing, especially with frameworks these days.

03:45.400 --> 03:47.880
The big disclaimer I always want to add at first is that

03:47.880 --> 03:51.560
vanilla JavaScript and browser APIs have come a long way.

03:51.560 --> 03:54.120
And so, if you haven't had a chance to check out some of,

03:54.120 --> 03:57.800
I think Mark has a post on redoing to do MVC and vanilla JavaScript,

03:57.800 --> 04:00.160
and so, you know, you might not need a framework.

04:00.160 --> 04:03.920
And I say that as a, you know, a core team member.

04:03.920 --> 04:06.880
But that said, Vue is designed from the ground up

04:06.880 --> 04:10.440
to be a reactive compiler optimized rendering system

04:10.440 --> 04:13.680
that rarely requires manual optimization.

04:13.680 --> 04:14.960
And so, when it comes to performance,

04:14.960 --> 04:16.360
especially the larger your apps get,

04:16.360 --> 04:19.440
a lot of times people are very curious about how we can

04:19.440 --> 04:22.520
basically prevent ourselves from creating bottlenecks in our performance

04:22.520 --> 04:25.720
because we don't understand maybe some of the core primitives about JavaScript,

04:25.720 --> 04:27.760
and that's what frameworks allow for us, right?

04:27.760 --> 04:29.080
They abstract some of that performance away,

04:29.080 --> 04:30.680
so they say, we'll take care of that,

04:30.680 --> 04:33.520
you just write your business logic, and we'll go from there.

04:34.800 --> 04:37.000
And so, you might have noticed too,

04:37.000 --> 04:39.960
I'm not putting any sort of charts or numbers as far as sizes go at this moment

04:39.960 --> 04:41.560
because one of the things that Vue,

04:41.560 --> 04:43.400
and I think a lot of the frameworks are doing right now,

04:43.400 --> 04:46.400
is that they're always trying to find ways to continue to be smaller, right?

04:46.400 --> 04:48.240
And they're learning from each other as we go.

04:48.240 --> 04:51.800
So, when you're deciding whether or not Vue is like the performance framework for you,

04:52.680 --> 04:54.920
especially if you're going to like migrate an enterprise level app,

04:54.920 --> 04:56.800
you're probably going to want to do some benchmarks of your own

04:56.800 --> 04:58.440
because they're going to be different contexts

04:58.440 --> 04:59.720
to why you might be making the decision

04:59.720 --> 05:02.680
because different bottlenecks exist within different contexts.

05:05.480 --> 05:08.840
Okay, the other thing is that Vue is versatile.

05:08.840 --> 05:09.880
So, what does that mean?

05:09.880 --> 05:12.880
So, via the docs, it's a rich, incremental,

05:12.880 --> 05:17.080
adoptable ecosystem that scales between a library and a full-featured framework.

05:17.080 --> 05:19.240
And so, for anyone who's gotten into the discussion,

05:19.240 --> 05:23.000
usually developers get very heated about the library versus framework argument, right?

05:23.000 --> 05:25.240
And that was the big thing for React 2, is React the library,

05:25.240 --> 05:26.160
when is it a framework.

05:27.160 --> 05:31.600
Basically, for Vue, what I would tell you is that it can be used basically as a CDN,

05:31.600 --> 05:33.800
basically as a drop-in library to your page,

05:33.800 --> 05:35.800
which we'll play around with just in a little bit,

05:35.800 --> 05:38.840
or it can be blown up to a fully framework project

05:38.840 --> 05:44.680
that you've come to get familiar with when it comes to front-end applications.

05:44.680 --> 05:46.920
And so, that's basically the difference, I would say, between the two.

05:46.920 --> 05:50.480
That one usually requires build tools, the other one does not.

05:50.480 --> 05:52.440
And so, Vue is really good at doing that.

05:52.440 --> 05:55.040
The other thing that drew me to Vue, especially the more I learned about it,

05:55.040 --> 05:56.920
is that it is community-first.

05:56.920 --> 05:59.800
Something that I think is often overlooked when it comes to Vue is that,

05:59.800 --> 06:02.640
currently, it's just basically within the big three of the frameworks, right?

06:02.640 --> 06:04.000
We have React, Vue, Angular.

06:05.320 --> 06:08.800
And if we think about it, React has Facebook backing it.

06:08.800 --> 06:11.200
We have Google backing Angular.

06:11.200 --> 06:13.840
Yet, Vue is a community-based open-source project

06:13.840 --> 06:16.960
that's managed to climb to the popularity that it has now.

06:16.960 --> 06:20.960
And I think that speaks a lot to the conventions and APIs that were designed

06:20.960 --> 06:23.320
when Vue was built as far as its approachability.

06:23.320 --> 06:26.080
I know people like to jump on the bandwagon

06:26.080 --> 06:28.120
as far as what big tech companies are doing,

06:28.120 --> 06:30.320
but then the fact that Vue was able to do this

06:30.320 --> 06:32.240
while basically learning from different frameworks

06:32.240 --> 06:35.120
and basically helping to do what's best for the community,

06:35.120 --> 06:37.320
I think that's always been a really attractive selling point of Vue,

06:37.320 --> 06:40.720
is because you know that it's driven by doing what's best for the community

06:40.720 --> 06:42.680
rather than what's best for the corporation.

06:42.680 --> 06:46.520
And we've seen trends of that over the community at times,

06:46.520 --> 06:48.520
but that's one of the things I really like about it.

06:49.800 --> 06:54.840
The other thing about Vue is that most of the core team

06:54.840 --> 06:57.160
is actually made up of volunteers across the world.

06:57.160 --> 07:00.360
So I think we have somewhat like 26 different countries of volunteers

07:00.360 --> 07:02.720
basically helping to maintain and build the project.

07:02.720 --> 07:06.040
And that provides for a kind of international perspective

07:06.080 --> 07:09.160
on what's happening in the world as far as different kind of apps.

07:09.160 --> 07:12.200
And so especially when people talk about numbers and popularity,

07:12.200 --> 07:14.360
a lot of things like NPM tend to take statistics

07:14.360 --> 07:16.520
from the Western hemisphere of the world,

07:16.520 --> 07:19.280
but a lot of things in Asia might not be included in that.

07:19.280 --> 07:21.760
And we do know that Vue is heavily used in Asia.

07:21.760 --> 07:24.600
And so it's really cool to see the different kind of perspectives

07:24.600 --> 07:25.600
that come with that.

07:25.600 --> 07:29.640
And so with that, we get a really sort of international mindset

07:29.640 --> 07:31.240
when it comes to building Vue.

07:32.240 --> 07:33.240
Okay.

07:34.240 --> 07:37.240
Of course, that said, people, when they think about Vue,

07:37.240 --> 07:40.240
because it's not backed by Google, it's not backed by Facebook,

07:40.240 --> 07:42.240
they want to know that it's enterprise proven.

07:42.240 --> 07:45.240
And granted, now that it's Vue 3, we've seen a lot of different apps.

07:45.240 --> 07:48.240
I want to just show a couple for those who might not be familiar with this,

07:48.240 --> 07:53.240
but Apple actually used Vue to build their introduction to SwiftUI.

07:53.240 --> 07:55.240
And so the way you know this, by the way,

07:55.240 --> 07:58.240
is if you install Vue DevTools, which I'll show you all how to do later,

07:58.240 --> 07:59.240
you'll notice in the upper right here,

07:59.240 --> 08:03.240
it'll actually detect when Vue is being used and basically flash green.

08:03.240 --> 08:07.240
Otherwise, by flash green, I mean it'll have that enabled state.

08:07.240 --> 08:08.240
Otherwise, it would be grayed out.

08:10.240 --> 08:11.240
And so here's another page.

08:11.240 --> 08:14.240
Here's the Google Careers page where they're also using Vue.

08:16.240 --> 08:19.240
And then some of you might have started using Zoom in the pandemic.

08:19.240 --> 08:21.240
And so, yep, Zoom also uses Vue.

08:23.240 --> 08:25.240
As far as e-commerce goes, we have REIs.

08:25.240 --> 08:28.240
So for all the outdoor people out there when you're using the e-commerce,

08:28.240 --> 08:30.240
Vue is powering that.

08:30.240 --> 08:32.240
L'Oreal Paris as well.

08:32.240 --> 08:34.240
We've had the team actually speak at some of the conferences before,

08:34.240 --> 08:37.240
really fascinating to learn about the different things they're doing

08:37.240 --> 08:38.240
when it comes to makeup.

08:38.240 --> 08:41.240
So some of the things they were doing were like interactive,

08:41.240 --> 08:44.240
basically like imagery where you could change the lipstick,

08:44.240 --> 08:47.240
and then it would change the image color of like the model and stuff like that.

08:47.240 --> 08:51.240
They were doing some really cool stuff as far as reactivity and that kind of thing.

08:52.240 --> 08:54.240
And then here's a fun one, Nintendo.

08:54.240 --> 08:56.240
Nintendo is powered by Vue as well.

08:56.240 --> 08:58.240
So if you're ever logging in, playing around with that,

08:58.240 --> 09:00.240
they are also using Vue.

09:02.240 --> 09:05.240
And if you didn't know, Media Wiki, which is like the big organization

09:05.240 --> 09:07.240
behind Wikipedia as well as some other properties,

09:07.240 --> 09:11.240
they actually did a really long investigation into deciding which framework

09:11.240 --> 09:15.240
they would adopt going forward for their basically migration.

09:15.240 --> 09:20.240
And basically, they landed on Vue, which we were really happy to hear.

09:20.240 --> 09:23.240
And so if you're ever looking to see kind of why they did that,

09:23.240 --> 09:25.240
there's a whole paper on that.

09:25.240 --> 09:27.240
So I just wanted to at least alert you all if you're curious about

09:27.240 --> 09:31.240
like a big organization migrating to Vue and their reasoning behind it.

09:31.240 --> 09:33.240
That is there for you all to read.

